{0 Type-safe router}

{1 Declaring routes}

Routes live in one manifest file compiled by [server-reason-react.router-gen].
The manifest is a single [Router.make] expression:

{[
Router.make(
  ~basePath="/demo/router",
  ~search={ text: Router.Search.optional(string) },
  ~layout=Pages.AppLayout,
  ~loading=Pages.GlobalLoading,
  ~error=Pages.AppError,
  ~notFound=Pages.NotFound,
  [
    Router.route(Home, ~page=Pages.App, ~path="/"),
    Router.route(NewNote, ~page=Pages.NewNote, ~path="/new", ~loading=Pages.NewNoteLoading),
    Router.group(
      ~path="/:id<NoteId.t>",
      ~layout=Pages.NoteLayout,
      ~loader=Pages.NoteLoader,
      ~loaderAs_=note,
      [
        Router.route(Note, ~page=Pages.Note, ~path="/", ~loading=Pages.NoteLoading),
        Router.route(EditNote, ~page=Pages.EditNote, ~path="/edit", ~loading=Pages.EditNoteLoading),
      ],
    ),
  ],
);
]}

[Router.route] declares a leaf page with a variant name. [Router.group] nests
routes under a shared path prefix and layout. Both accept attachments, as does
the root.

[Router.redirect] declares an inbound temporary redirect without adding a
route constructor or link helper to the generated application API:

{[
Router.redirect(
  ~path="/old-notes/:id<NoteId.t>",
  ~to_=(~id, ()) => Router.Note.destination(~id, ()),
)
]}

The destination callback receives the redirect's decoded path and search
values, including values inherited from parent groups and the root. Redirects
inside groups inherit their path and search schemas, but do not run group
loaders or attachments.

Use [Router.unsafeDestination] when the target is outside the generated route
manifest or must preserve a legacy URL exactly:

{[
Router.redirect(
  ~path="/docs",
  ~to_=() => Router.unsafeDestination("https://docs.example.com"),
)
]}

[unsafeDestination] does not validate or encode its argument. Prefer a
generated route destination when the router owns the target URL.

Attachments take modules, and the generator resolves the convention inside
each module:

- [~page], [~layout], [~loading], [~notFound], [~invalidSearch] resolve
  [Module.make], a component.
- [~loader] resolves [Module.load]. The scope's components receive the loader
  result under the module name ([Pages.NoteLoader] becomes [~noteLoader]), or
  under the [~loaderAs_] name when given.
- [~error] at the root resolves an error policy: a module with a [status]
  function and a [make] component. On groups and routes, [~error] is a boundary
  component.
- [~metadata] and [~headers] are plain functions, not modules.

{2 Path syntax}

[~path] is an absolute path made of slash-separated segments. Use ["/"] for an
empty path. A group path is a prefix for its child group and route paths, so
["/notes"] and ["/:id<int>"] compose to ["/notes/:id<int>"]. Each path must
start with [/]. Empty segments, trailing slashes, [.] segments, and [..]
segments are invalid.

A segment is one of:

- A static value such as [notes], [new], or [café]. Write decoded text in the
  manifest. The router percent-encodes it when it builds an href. A static
  segment can contain letters, digits, valid UTF-8, and URI path punctuation,
  but it cannot contain [/], [%], [?], or [#].
- A typed parameter in the form [:name<type>]. The parameter must occupy the
  complete segment. The name follows Reason labelled-argument syntax. [bool],
  [int], and [string] are built in. A custom type must have the form
  [Module.t].
- A final catch-all in the form [:name<string...>]. It matches one or more
  remaining segments and the page receives them as [list(string)]. It must be
  the last segment of the route path, it cannot appear in a group path, and it
  never matches zero segments: [/assets/:parts<string...>] does not match
  [/assets]. Declare a separate [/assets] route when both forms exist.

Optional parameters and mixed segments such as [file-:id<int>] are not
supported. Put search values after [?] in a destination through [~search],
rather than in [~path].

[~basePath] uses the static path syntax and cannot contain parameters. The
registry rejects duplicate paths and patterns that can match the same pathname
with equal specificity. This includes crossing catch-all patterns such as
[/foo/:left<string...>] and [/:scope<string>/bar/:right<string...>]. When an
overlap has a unique winner, the pattern with more static segments takes
precedence, and a route without a catch-all takes precedence over a catch-all
route with the same static count. When one route path is a prefix of another,
parameters at the same position must use the same name and type.

{3 Trailing slashes}

Generated hrefs never end with a slash, so the canonical form of every route
is slash-free. [~trailingSlash] on [Router.make] sets the policy for request
pathnames that end with one or more slashes:

- [Redirect], the default, answers with a permanent redirect to the canonical
  pathname. Document requests receive status 308, so the request method
  survives the redirect. The query string is preserved.
- [Reject] treats the request as not found.

{3 Path parameter codecs}

The router decodes a segment written [:name<Module.t>] with [Module.parse]
and prints it with [Module.to_string]:

{[
/* NoteId.re */
type t = int;
let parse = value =>
  switch (int_of_string_opt(value)) {
  | Some(id) when id >= 0 => Ok(id)
  | _ => Error("expected a note id")
  };
let to_string = string_of_int;
]}

Codecs receive percent-decoded segments. The router owns URL encoding. [bool],
[int], and [string] are built in. [bool] accepts exactly [true] and [false].
[to_string] must return one non-empty, valid UTF-8 path segment. Destination
construction raises [Invalid_argument] for an empty value, [/], [.], [..],
control characters, or invalid UTF-8. Valid output is percent-encoded. A failed
parse produces [InvalidPathParameter].

{2 Search parameters}

Any scope declares search parameters as a typed record:

{[
~search={
  page: Router.Search.default(int, 1),
  filter: Router.Search.optional(Filter.t),
  tags: Router.Search.many(string),
  token: Router.Search.required(string),
}
]}

Child scopes see their ancestors' parameters as well as their own. Canonical
URLs omit values that equal their declared default. A required root search
parameter together with a root [~error] policy also requires [~invalidSearch].

{1 Generated application API}

Each route is a component and owns its typed helpers:

{[
<Router.Note id> children </Router.Note>

let destination = Router.Note.destination(~id, ())
let href = Router.Note.href(~id, ())

switch Router.useRoute() {
| Some(Router.Note({id})) => React.string(NoteId.to_string(id))
| _ => React.null
}
]}

[Router.useRoute] returns the active route as a generated variant with decoded
path parameters. It returns [None] when no route matched.

{2 Navigation}

[Router.useNavigation] returns the imperative navigate function and the
current navigation status:

{[
let (navigate, status) = Router.useNavigation();

let onSelect = id =>
  navigate(Router.Note.destination(~id, ())) |> ignore;

let isPending =
  switch (status) {
  | Router.Navigation.Loading(_) => true
  | Idle
  | Failed(_) => false
  };
]}

Navigating to a destination is a {e content} navigation. The client requests a
Flight model and the server answers with a full model, or with a partial patch
that reuses unchanged layouts. [~revalidate=true] forces a full response.
[~history] selects [Push], [Replace], or [Pop].

{2 Search updates}

[Router.useSearch] returns the decoded search record and its typed update
function:

{[
let ({Router.text: text}, updateSearch) = Router.useSearch();

let onChange = value => updateSearch(~text=Some(value), ()) |> ignore;
]}

Search updates are {e shallow}. They update the URL and client state
synchronously and never contact the server. When server-rendered content
depends on a search value, navigate to a destination carrying the new value
instead of calling [updateSearch].

[Router.useUpdateHash] commits hash-only changes without a request.

{1 Loaders}

A loader runs on the server before streaming. It receives the decoded path
parameters, the scope's search values, and ancestor loader results as labelled
arguments, and returns a [Router.Loader.result]:

{[
module NoteLoader = {
  let load = (~id, ~text as _, ()) =>
    DB.fetchNoteOption(id)
    |> Lwt.map(result =>
         switch (result) {
         | Ok(Some(note)) => Router.Loader.Data(note)
         | Ok(None) => Router.Loader.NotFound
         | Error(error) => Router.Loader.Error(error)
         }
       );
};
]}

Loaders execute parent-first and complete before response headers or RSC
rendering begin. The scope's components receive [Data] ([~note] in the
manifest above). [NotFound] and [Error] render the nearest boundary, and
[Redirect(destination)] escapes the whole branch.

A loader marks its scope and all descendants as non-reusable for navigation
patches. Keep loaders for fast validation, redirects, and route-level
not-found decisions; stream slow data from async server components inside the
route elements instead.

{1 Error handling}

The root [~error] policy maps application errors to statuses and renders them:

{[
module AppError = {
  let status = _ => Router.Status.InternalServerError;

  [@react.component]
  let make = (~text as _, ~error as _) =>
    <p> "The requested note could not be loaded." </p>;
};
]}

[Router.Error.t] distinguishes [NotFound], [Application], invalid path or
search parameters, and [Internal] failures carrying only a diagnostic id.
Boundary components receive the scope inputs plus [~error]. The nearest
[~notFound] handles missing routes and loader [NotFound] results. Without one,
the nearest [~error] renders instead. A path or search decode failure in a
nested scope renders the nearest ancestor [~error] boundary before any loader
at or below the failing scope runs. Root search failures use [~invalidSearch]
when declared.

{1 Metadata and headers}

[~metadata] and [~headers] resolve per scope on every response, including
patches, and merge from the root down. Like loaders, they receive the decoded
path parameters, search values, and ancestor loader results, and return
[Lwt] promises:

{[
module NoteMetadata = {
  let make = (~id as _, ~text as _, ~note, ()) =>
    Lwt.return(Router.Metadata.make(~title=Note.title(note), ()));
};
]}

{1 Server integration}

The native generator emits [RouterRegistry.server], a configured
[RouterServer.Server.t]. A native HTTP adapter builds a
[RouterServer.ServerEngine.request] from the request location and router
navigation headers, then calls [RouterServer.Server.run]. The adapter maps the
resulting full, patch, redirect, permanent-redirect, or reload-required outcome
to its HTTP server and streams render data through [ReactServerDOM].

The repository's [demo/dream-router/DreamRouter.ml] implements this boundary
for Dream and can be used as an adapter reference. [DreamRouter] is demo-only:
it is not installed by any published package.

{1 Libraries}

Melange applications depend on [server-reason-react.router]. Native
applications depend on [server-reason-react.router-native]. Route generation
uses [server-reason-react.router-gen]. A native adapter that calls
[ReactServerDOM] also depends on [server-reason-react.reactDom]. There is no
installed Dream router package.
